olling available, uncertainty stays wide but the favorite is clear.", "watch": "If Clark converts his active debate schedule and the sense that the race is closer than expected into measurable movement among swing voters, the margin could tighten.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Tom Tiffany (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Tom Tiffany's retirement leaves Wisconsin's 7th District open, but the seat's Republican fundamentals remain intact. The district leans about eleven points Republican at the presidential level, and every major race rating calls the contest Solid or Safe Republican, with only one outlet venturing Likely R. Prediction markets price a Republican win at roughly 94 percent. No public general-election polls are available, so the forecast rests on fundamentals, ratings, and money.\n\nRepublican Michael Alfonso has raised about $1.35 million and benefits from roughly $2.2 million in outside support, dwarfing Democrat Fred Clark's $423,000 in receipts. Clark has campaigned actively, and the nominees have met in several debates covering Line 5, data centers, and the expired Farm Bill. A progressive opinion column argues the race is closer than it should be, though no polling yet substantiates a genuine toss-up. The seat remains favored to stay Republican.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0554, "margin": -10.68}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0396, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0389, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0525, "margin": -12.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0215, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0228, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.027, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0434, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0445, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0261, "margin": -14.0}, {"date"